WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.570
A nation's most precious possession was the land it had spent centuries stealing from the sea.

2
00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:12.260
In the summer of sixteen seventy-two, the Dutch decided that the only way to save that land was to drown it.

3
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:18.790
That same week, they tore apart, in the open street, the man who had governed the country for twenty years.

4
00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:32.880
In the spring of sixteen seventy-two, the richest country in Europe was also one of the smallest.

5
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:43.890
The United Provinces of the Dutch Republic, seven provinces in all, covered no more ground than a medium-sized province does today, and its population was a little over two million;

6
00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:54.420
yet in the harbour of Amsterdam the masts of anchored ships rose into the sky like a forest, and on the city's exchange the price of everything from Baltic wheat to Javanese pepper was settled.

7
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.910
This republic had no king.

8
00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:07.960
When the young stadholder died of smallpox in sixteen fifty, he left behind an unborn son, and the merchant families of the towns decided never to fill that vacancy again.

9
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:13.050
For twenty-two years the country was governed by assemblies, clerks and ledgers.

10
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:23.900
The architect of that order was Johan de Witt, chief administrator of the province of Holland since sixteen fifty-three: a mathematician, the author of a book on the calculus of chances,

11
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:29.310
the first man to work out correctly the true value of a contract for a life annuity.

12
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.480
His strategy was simple and consistent.

13
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:41.090
The money of a maritime state goes to the navy; an army is expensive, dangerous, and hands political power to its commanders.

14
00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:47.460
There was one flaw in this calculation: Louis the Fourteenth of France had no intention of forgiving the Dutch.

15
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:55.750
They had stood at the centre of the triple alliance that in sixteen sixty-eight forced him to break off his conquests in the Spanish Netherlands halfway through.

16
00:01:55.750 --> 00:02:00.200
The tariff walls of his minister Colbert had failed to crush Dutch trade.

17
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:07.530
On top of that, the printing houses of Amsterdam were turning out pamphlets, medals and caricatures that mocked the French king.

18
00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:17.420
In sixteen seventy, Louis signed a secret treaty with Charles the Second of England at Dover: in exchange for French money, the English navy would go to war against the Dutch.

19
00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:24.590
Then two German church states, the prince-bishoprics of Münster and Cologne, were bought with the same French gold.

20
00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:28.240
Four enemies, one plan of attack.

21
00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.730
The first blow came before any declaration.

22
00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:42.580
On the twelfth of March, English ships fell upon the Dutch merchant convoy returning from Smyrna in the Channel; the declaration of war followed two weeks later, on the twenty-seventh of March.

23
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.590
France declared on the sixth of April.

24
00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:53.240
In May an army of a hundred and twenty thousand men gathered near Liège, with Turenne, Condé and the king himself at its head.

25
00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:59.130
A separate force of thirty thousand, hired by Münster and Cologne, would advance from the east.

26
00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:11.260
On the other side there was, on paper, a Dutch army of as many as eighty thousand, but most units stood at half strength, officers' commissions had become salary lines distributed among town families,

27
00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:16.510
and the bulk of the soldiers were waiting in the southern fortresses, in the wrong place.

28
00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:27.200
In February, in desperation, the boy born fatherless in sixteen fifty was brought forward: William, twenty-one years old, was made captain-general of the army.

29
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:34.850
The Perpetual Edict of sixteen sixty-seven, drawn up to keep him away from that office for ever, was forgotten five years later.

30
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:44.260
On the morning of the twelfth of June the French army crossed the lower branch of the Rhine in front of an old customs house called the Tolhuis, near the village of Lobith.

31
00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:51.430
The river was low that summer; the cavalry rode across a shallow ford and the infantry were carried over on pontoons.

32
00:03:51.430 --> 00:04:01.800
At Versailles this crossing was worked for years into paintings, ceiling frescoes and reliefs; the scene, known as the Crossing of the Rhine, was sold as the king's greatest feat of arms.

33
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:07.210
In fact the losses were almost nil, because there was no one facing them.

34
00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:12.620
The defensive line built along the river IJssel was turned from behind in a single stroke.

35
00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:19.790
In the weeks that followed Doesburg, Zutphen, Deventer, Arnhem and Nijmegen fell one after another.

36
00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:23.440
The fortresses held out not for days but for hours.

37
00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:31.410
At the end of June the city of Utrecht opened its gates without firing a shot, and its council went out to receive the king of France.

38
00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:37.460
Three provinces were lost in six weeks, without an army even having to be beaten in the field.

39
00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:42.710
The people fleeing back poured in floods into the towns of the province of Holland.

40
00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:52.760
Queues formed outside Amsterdam's city bank as depositors tried to withdraw their money, bond prices collapsed, days of public repentance were proclaimed in the churches.

41
00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:57.530
In the street, the name of Johan de Witt was now spoken together with treason.

42
00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:05.340
The Dutch would later remember that year in three rhyming words: the people irrational, the government helpless, the country beyond saving.

43
00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:10.110
The only weapon left was the thing they had regarded as an enemy for centuries.

44
00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:13.600
Most of the province of Holland lies below sea level.

45
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:22.210
This land was an artificial landscape, won from marsh over centuries, ringed with dikes, drained by windmills, encircled by canals.

46
00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:29.220
Every polder, every drained basin inside its ring of dikes, was in fact a machine that could be run in reverse.

47
00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:35.270
If you opened the sluices of the system that pushed the water out, the same system would let the water back in.

48
00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:39.720
The decision was taken on the eighth of June by the States of Holland.

49
00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:47.210
A strip running from Gorinchem in the south to Muiden in the north, on the shore of the Zuiderzee, was to be put under water.

50
00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:53.740
This line would later be known as the Old Dutch Water Line, and its logic demanded a merciless precision.

51
00:05:53.740 --> 00:06:01.710
If the water rose too high, a boat could cross it; if it rose too little, soldiers would simply walk through.

52
00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:05.360
The depth they were after was roughly thirty centimetres.

53
00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.010
A sheet of water above a man's heel and below his knee.

54
00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:15.700
Under such a cover the ground becomes invisible; beneath it lie hidden ditches, irrigation channels, pits of mud.

55
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:23.830
Horses dragging heavy gun carriages sink into them, infantry lose formation, an engineer cannot tell where to throw a bridge.

56
00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:32.120
The only dry passages were the raised roads and the crowns of the dikes; a redoubt was built at the head of each one and guns were placed there.

57
00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:35.770
Opening the sluices was harder than taking the decision.

58
00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:40.540
That ground was somebody's pasture, somebody's hay waiting to be cut.

59
00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:49.790
It was the middle of June; asked to drown their own fields in the middle of the harvest, farmers went out at night and stopped up the breaches in the dikes with brushwood and earth.

60
00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:55.040
Some town governments delayed the order, others sat down to bargain over compensation.

61
00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:01.410
Soldiers were posted as sentries at the dikes, with permission to open fire on anyone closing a gap.

62
00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:08.580
Day after day the water was raised, measured afresh each morning, sluice by sluice and breach by breach.

63
00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:12.550
There was one moment when all of it came very close to being wasted.

64
00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:19.400
The little town of Muiden, just east of Amsterdam, held the great sea sluice at the heart of the whole system.

65
00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:26.890
On the twentieth of June a French cavalry detachment reached the town; the gates stood open and there was no garrison.

66
00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:37.900
The detachment rode in, looked around a little, saw that no one was expecting them, and rode back out; it had neither an officer who understood the value of what he was holding, nor orders to stay.

67
00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:41.230
Dutch soldiers reached Muiden only hours later.

68
00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:47.440
Had that sluice remained in French hands, flooding the northern flank of Amsterdam would have been impossible.

69
00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:57.010
On the twenty-second of June the water took its place, and the French army came to a halt forty kilometres west of Utrecht, on the shore of a sea of mud it could not cross.

70
00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:00.340
Louis was in no hurry at that point.

71
00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:05.590
He preferred to dictate terms of surrender to a country he considered militarily finished.

72
00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:17.400
The terms were read out to the Dutch delegation summoned to Utrecht: the great fortresses in the south would be handed to France, Catholic worship would be permitted, millions of guilders in war indemnity would be paid,

73
00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:27.130
the republic would from then on enter no alliance without French approval, and every year a special embassy would travel to Versailles to present the king with a medal of gratitude.

74
00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:32.060
The last article was not a military demand but a declaration of ownership.

75
00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:39.550
When the terms became known in The Hague, public opinion exploded; what people talked about was not the money on offer but the medal.

76
00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:48.960
The answer attributed to the young captain-general, William, was handed down years later by the pen of an English historian: told that he was doomed to see the end of his country,

77
00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.730
he said there was a way of not seeing it, and that was to die in the last ditch.

78
00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:57.380
The same weapon was used a second time in the north.

79
00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:08.870
Bernhard von Galen, prince-bishop of Münster, whom the Dutch called Bomber Berend for his passion for siege guns, arrived before the city of Groningen at the end of July with more than twenty thousand men.

80
00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:15.720
The defence of the city was directed by Carl von Rabenhaupt, an old Bohemian soldier in the service of the republic.

81
00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:23.370
He too flooded the surrounding marshes and fields, condemned the siege lines to mud, and sent raiding parties against the trenches by night.

82
00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:29.420
The bishop threw thousands of bombs into the city; he brought down stone walls, but not the will behind them.

83
00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:33.390
On the twenty-eighth of August he lifted the siege and withdrew.

84
00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:36.880
Groningen still celebrates that day every year.

85
00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:41.970
The north and the west of the country had survived by sacrificing their own soil.

86
00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:48.820
But in those same weeks, in the political centre of the republic, something was happening that no enemy had done.

87
00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:56.470
Johan de Witt was the man who had governed this country for nineteen years, and the country he governed had decided to pin the label of treason on him.

88
00:09:56.470 --> 00:10:07.320
He came from an old family of Dordrecht, he had studied law and mathematics at Leiden, and the book he wrote on the algebraic treatment of curves was taken seriously among the mathematicians of his day.

89
00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:15.930
His political creed was built like a theorem as well: true freedom meant government by the shared reason of the towns, not by a single dynasty.

90
00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:26.140
The most brilliant fruit of that creed had been gathered in sixteen sixty-seven, when the Dutch fleet sailed into the branches of the river Thames, burned part of the English fleet in its own harbour,

91
00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:29.630
and forced England to peace.

92
00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:36.800
The man aboard on that raid, as the States' representative with the fleet, was Johan's elder brother Cornelis de Witt.

93
00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.770
Five years earlier the brothers had been national heroes.

94
00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:48.580
In sixteen seventy-two the very same choice, money going to the ships and the land army left unmaintained, became their death warrant.

95
00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:58.310
Within weeks the presses of Amsterdam and The Hague turned out hundreds of pamphlets: the de Witt brothers had made a deal with the French, they had deliberately left the fortresses empty,

96
00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:03.560
they had kept the young prince away on purpose from the office that was his by right.

97
00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:12.810
On the evening of the twenty-first of June, while the French army waited in front of the water line, four young men stabbed Johan de Witt near his house in The Hague.

98
00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:20.780
One of the attackers, Jacob van der Graeff, was caught and executed within weeks; part of the crowd began visiting his grave.

99
00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:25.230
The gravely wounded chief administrator could not leave his bed for weeks.

100
00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:29.840
In the country's most critical days, the helm of the state stood empty.

101
00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:32.690
The vacancy filled quickly.

102
00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:44.660
At the beginning of July, under pressure from crowds besieging the town councils, the States of Holland repealed the Perpetual Edict of sixteen sixty-seven, and on the third of July proclaimed William stadholder.

103
00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:49.110
Twenty-two years on, the republic annulled its own founding decision.

104
00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:52.920
For the brothers, this was not enough.

105
00:11:52.920 --> 00:12:00.730
A barber surgeon from Piershil named Willem Tichelaar came forward and claimed that Cornelis de Witt had offered him money to kill the prince.

106
00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:05.980
There was no evidence and no witness; the only thing on the table was the barber's word.

107
00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:11.870
Cornelis was arrested, shut up in the town prison in The Hague and put to torture to make him confess.

108
00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:20.640
According to the story handed down, through hours of torture he repeated Horace's famous lines in praise of the just and steadfast man, and signed nothing.

109
00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:25.250
The court could not sentence him to death; it sentenced him to exile.

110
00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:29.700
At the beginning of August, Johan de Witt resigned from all his offices.

111
00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:38.790
On the morning of the twentieth of August, Johan went to that prison, the gate prison of the city known as the Gevangenpoort, to collect the brother who was to go into exile.

112
00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:45.480
A crowd gathered in front of it before long; the city's militia companies took up positions in the square under arms.

113
00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:53.770
The cavalry regiment, the only force capable of keeping order in The Hague, had been sent out that day to put down a disturbance outside the city,

114
00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:58.220
and its commander announced that he would not return without an explicit order.

115
00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:01.070
In the afternoon the crowd broke down the door.

116
00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:05.040
The two brothers were dragged into the street and killed.

117
00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:12.530
Their bodies were hung head down from the gallows opposite, torn apart, and passed from hand to hand through the crowd as souvenirs.

118
00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:16.500
Johan was forty-six, Cornelis forty-nine.

119
00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:19.830
Afterwards there was no inquiry and no trial.

120
00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:26.680
Some of the leading figures of that day were rewarded in later years with offices and incomes, the barber surgeon among them.

121
00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:37.690
What the young stadholder knew that day, and what he was in a position to stop and did not stop, is still debated among historians; what is certain is that he asked for no penalty for the killers.

122
00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:45.180
The rift between the merchant government of the republic and the House of Orange took, in that street, a shape that would last two centuries.

123
00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:53.150
While all this was happening, the real fate of the war was being settled not in The Hague but at sea, and had been since two weeks before.

124
00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:56.640
The water line had stopped the army coming by land.

125
00:13:56.640 --> 00:14:06.050
But the real part of the plan was this: the English and French fleets would join, land troops on the Dutch coast, and take the cities behind the line from the sea.

126
00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:16.260
Against that plan the republic had a man of sixty-five, Michiel de Ruyter, who had gone to sea at nine years old as a rope worker and risen from ship's captain to admiral.

127
00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:24.230
The force in his hands was some seventy-five ships, around four thousand five hundred guns and close to twenty-one thousand men.

128
00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:35.880
The combined fleet facing him was larger; its ships of the line alone numbered more than eighty, it carried over six thousand guns, and at its head was the brother of the king of England, James, Duke of York.

129
00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:38.090
De Ruyter did not wait.

130
00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:48.620
On the morning of the seventh of June he found the allied fleet on the east coast of England, in Sole Bay off Suffolk: ships at anchor, taking on water and provisions, part of the crews ashore.

131
00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:51.790
He took the wind behind him and drove into the bay.

132
00:14:51.790 --> 00:15:01.520
The allied line was split before it could form; the French division, by the reading of its admiral d'Estrées, stood away to the south and spent most of the day out of the battle.

133
00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:08.530
Seeing this, De Ruyter detached a quarter of his fleet to keep an eye on the French and threw the rest in a mass against the English.

134
00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:20.180
In a close-range gun duel that lasted until evening both sides took heavy losses; the hundred-gun flagship of the Earl of Sandwich, admiral of the English blue squadron, was set alight by fireships,

135
00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:23.190
the ship blew up and the admiral died.

136
00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:26.680
The next day both sides declared victory.

137
00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:33.690
But the allied fleet had to return to port for repairs, and not one soldier was landed on the Dutch coast that summer.

138
00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:37.500
The next threat came not from the wind but from the temperature.

139
00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:44.190
The flaw in the water line was obvious: if thirty centimetres of water freezes, an army walks over it.

140
00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:56.000
In December of sixteen seventy-two a hard frost set in, and in those days William the Third had taken the greater part of the field army south, towards the French fortress of Charleroi;

141
00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:59.170
the line was being held by a thin screen.

142
00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:06.180
The French commander in Utrecht, the Duke of Luxembourg, saw his chance and marched west over the ice with picked troops.

143
00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:08.550
The target was The Hague.

144
00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:15.880
As they passed the villages of Bodegraven and Zwammerdam on the line, the weather turned at midnight and the thaw began.

145
00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:22.090
The ice started to crack under them and the roads began to turn to swamp; they were forced to fall back.

146
00:16:22.090 --> 00:16:30.700
On the way back, between the twenty-eighth and the thirtieth of December, they burned those two villages and used severe violence against the inhabitants.

147
00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:40.270
In the hands of the Dutch presses the episode became the most widely printed image of the war: the village in flames, families running, soldiers' boots.

148
00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:46.960
The French threat was no longer an abstract military problem but an engraving that could be hung on the wall of every house.

149
00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:51.570
In the north, in those same days, the ice worked for the Dutch instead.

150
00:16:51.570 --> 00:17:02.580
Rabenhaupt, who had defended Groningen, raided the fortress of Coevorden across the frozen ditches on the thirtieth of December and took back this key point that Münster had captured in the summer.

151
00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:07.830
Before the winter was over, the eastern flank of the occupation was beginning to come apart.

152
00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:12.280
In sixteen seventy-three the matter was closed for good at sea.

153
00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:21.690
The English and French fleet came this time under the command of Prince Rupert, with transports carrying soldiers alongside it; the intention was again to make a landing.

154
00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:30.940
In June De Ruyter turned them back twice in the Schooneveld shoals, on the seventh and the fourteenth of June, using the shallow water of his own coast like a fortress.

155
00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:34.590
On the twenty-first of August, off Texel,

156
00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:44.480
he fought an all-day battle against a numerically superior fleet and at the same time got through the merchant convoy returning from the East Indies with a year's revenue of the republic in its holds.

157
00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:48.770
The landing troops waited aboard their ships and were never put ashore.

158
00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:52.900
The republic had survived because it had not lost the sea.

159
00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:57.030
By sixteen seventy-three the ledger of Europe had changed.

160
00:17:57.030 --> 00:18:03.880
France swallowing a small merchant republic in six weeks woke Madrid, Vienna and Berlin from their sleep.

161
00:18:03.880 --> 00:18:13.610
William the Third's envoys made good use of this; Spain, the Holy Roman Emperor and Brandenburg entered the war on the Dutch side one after another.

162
00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:20.140
The young stadholder was no longer alone, and for the first time he could attack at a place of his own choosing.

163
00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:28.590
In September he retook the fortress of Naarden, which held the eastern gate of Amsterdam; this was his army's first real success in a year.

164
00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:31.600
Then he did something nobody expected.

165
00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:41.170
Instead of grappling with the French army inside Holland, he joined the imperial and Spanish forces and marched southeast, up the Rhine, and in November took the city of Bonn.

166
00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:46.580
Bonn was the lock on the line by which the French army in Utrecht was supplied along the river.

167
00:18:46.580 --> 00:18:53.270
When the lock was broken, the occupying force inside the country found itself suddenly without supply and surrounded.

168
00:18:53.270 --> 00:19:00.600
In the spring of sixteen seventy-four the French evacuated Utrecht and the eastern provinces without fighting a battle worth the name.

169
00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:06.010
As the coalition began to come apart, the enemies came to the table one by one.

170
00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:15.260
England left the war with the Treaty of Westminster, signed on the nineteenth of February, sixteen seventy-four; Münster and Cologne made peace the same year.

171
00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:23.550
The war with France lasted until sixteen seventy-eight and ended with the Peace of Nijmegen; the republic came out of it without losing territory.

172
00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:26.720
On paper, it was a war that had been won.

173
00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:28.930
Under the paper lay the bill.

174
00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:34.980
The water line had been the saviour, but the ones who paid for it were the people who lived under that water.

175
00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:47.110
The salt water let in from the Zuiderzee left meadows that gave no crop for years; dikes, mills and farm buildings were wrecked; in the occupied provinces French commanders had loaded the towns with heavy cash levies.

176
00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:53.640
The state debt multiplied during the war years and never came back down to the level it had held before.

177
00:19:53.640 --> 00:20:03.530
Historians do not agree on exactly where the Dutch Golden Age ended, but there is broad agreement that the country's commercial momentum never regained its old speed after sixteen seventy-two.

178
00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:07.500
The republic kept its existence and lost its primacy.

179
00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:12.430
And the longest shadow of that year fell somewhere nobody could have predicted that spring.

180
00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:19.440
In sixteen seventy-two a twenty-one-year-old with no office had come to power because a country was being sunk under water.

181
00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:25.810
In sixteen seventy-seven he married Mary Stuart, the daughter of his uncle and heir to the English throne.

182
00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:36.020
In November of sixteen eighty-eight, at the invitation of the English opposition, he crossed the Channel with a fleet of hundreds of ships and landed troops at Torbay in the southwest of England.

183
00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:44.470
His father-in-law James the Second fled the country; in the spring of sixteen eighty-nine William and Mary sat down together on the throne of England.

184
00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:50.360
One of the two navies that had joined sixteen years earlier to drown his country was now under his command.

185
00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:59.130
That Louis the Fourteenth would fight all his remaining wars with the same man in front of him was the long-term price of not having been in a hurry at Utrecht in sixteen seventy-two.

186
00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:03.260
The water itself remained at the centre of Dutch military thinking.

187
00:21:03.260 --> 00:21:14.110
The dike breaches hastily cut in the seventeenth century turned over the following two centuries into a system that was a marvel of engineering: calculated sluices, measured canals,

188
00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:17.440
hundreds of redoubts and forts strung along the line.

189
00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:28.450
This arrangement, known as the New Dutch Water Line, was still standing when it was called into service for the last time in May of nineteen forty, and it was still designed to hold the water at thirty centimetres;

190
00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:31.620
but in that war the enemy flew over the water.

191
00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:42.790
Today the line's dikes, sluice houses and redoubts are on the UNESCO World Heritage list; cyclists ride along the dikes between the pastures, and cafés operate inside the forts.

192
00:21:42.790 --> 00:21:46.280
War does not occur to anyone looking at that scene.

193
00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:54.090
Yet underneath it lies this decision: what a country does when it understands that it can defend itself only by ruining its own land.

194
00:21:54.090 --> 00:21:59.500
In sixteen seventy-two the Dutch answered that question, and opened the sluices.

195
00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:03.150
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

196
00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:06.800
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
